avorite. National coverage points to a mixed political climate, but nothing in the evidence suggests a shift large enough to put the state's electoral votes in play.", "watch": "A decisive national Democratic wave could narrow the margin, though flipping the state would require a historic partisan realignment.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Dakota remains one of the most Republican states in presidential voting, with a partisan lean of roughly eighteen points toward the GOP. The incumbent president is term-limited, so neither side benefits from incumbency, and no general-election polls or expert ratings are available for this race. With no direct polling, the picture rests on the state's fundamental partisan balance.\n\nWithout surveys to refine the estimate, uncertainty is wider than usual, but the structural advantage for the Republican nominee is substantial. Recent national coverage suggests a mixed environment heading into the election, yet there is no sign of a shift large enough to make North Dakota competitive. The Republican nominee is a heavy favorite, and the realistic question is the size of the margin rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0273, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0558, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0549, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0572, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0505, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0515, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0556, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0598, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0528, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0566, "margin": -